\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The oscillator Hamiltonian}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the normalized transfer operator
#{\widehat T=T/\lambda_0} of \ref{fdip-007U}, the
\newvocab{transfer Hamiltonian} is the spectral logarithm
#{H=-\log\widehat T}. Explicitly, if
#{f=\sum_{n\ge0}c_nh_n^s}, its domain and action are
##{\begin{aligned}
D(H)&=\left\{f:\sum_{n=0}^\infty
                    (n\omega)^2|c_n|^2<\infty\right\},\\
Hf&=\sum_{n=0}^\infty n\omega c_nh_n^s.
\end{aligned}}
It is nonnegative and self-adjoint, with eigenvalues #{n\omega}.
Its zero eigenspace is the line spanned by #{h_0^s}, and its mass
gap is #{\omega}. It is unbounded even though
#{\widehat T} is bounded and injective. The operator #{H} generates
this Euclidean evolution. Continuation to real time changes the
Euclidean real form to a Lorentzian one. The two real forms stay
distinct.}

\p{On the finite Hermite span, it has the differential expression
##{Hf=\frac\omega{2s}
 \left(-f''+s^2x^2f-sf\right).}
The closure of this expression on that span is the above spectral
operator. This is the harmonic oscillator with its ground energy
subtracted. The time step has length one; a specified step
#{a_t>0} instead gives #{H/a_t} and gap #{\omega /a_t}.}

\proof{
\p{The unitary coefficient map #{f\mapsto(c_n)} identifies
#{\widehat T} with multiplication by #{q^n>0} on #{\ell^2}.
Its negative logarithm is multiplication by #{n\omega} on the
stated maximal domain. A real diagonal operator on that domain
is self-adjoint; its quadratic form is nonnegative. The listed
eigenspaces, spectrum and gap follow directly, and
#{\|Hh_n^s\|=n\omega} proves unboundedness. Finite coefficient
truncations converge both in norm and after applying #{H}, so
the finite Hermite span is a core.}

\p{Differentiating the Hermite generating function and comparing
coefficients gives
#{\operatorname{He}_n''(u)-u\operatorname{He}_n'(u)
+n\operatorname{He}_n(u)=0}. Substitution into the definition of
#{h_n^s} yields
##{-(h_n^s)''+s^2x^2h_n^s=(2n+1)s\,h_n^s.}
The displayed differential expression therefore acts as
#{n\omega} on every basis vector. The core statement proves
that its closure is exactly #{H}.}
}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.hermiteFunction_oscillator}
proves the pointwise differential identity at #{s=1} for the
same normalized Hermite functions. Dilation gives the identity
above for #{s>0}; the spectral logarithm, its domain and the
closure statement are supplied by the preceding argument.}

\p{The logarithmic prescription follows
\citet{Eq. (5.5), p. 4}{wingate2025lattice}, with the leading
transfer eigenvalue removed explicitly. It constructs the
Hamiltonian for a Gaussian model of finite spatial volume.
No limit in time, spatial volume or lattice spacing is needed.}
